<h2><a id="Presentació" name="Presentació"></a>Presentation and options</h2>

<p>This application converts structured vector files containing points, arcs, polygons, or nodes (PNT, ARC, POL, or NOD formats) and unstructured vector files containing points, lines, or polygons (VEC format) to DXF format, which can be imported into AutoCAD (see the DXFIN command in the AutoCAD manual) and other applications, such as MicroStation, CorelDraw, or MS Word. The application also exports the third dimension (Z coordinate), if present in the source formats.</p>

<p>The application provides two options:</p>

<!--This section contains the MSA options and must be edited in the WPS document (aplicacio.mps).-->
<span id="wpsopcions"/>
<!--End of the MSA options section editable in WPS.-->

<p>Polygons are converted to closed polylines; lines and arcs are converted to open polylines; and points and nodes can be converted to either points or text. The maximum number of vertices per line or polygon is 4.2 million.</p>

<p>The generated DXF file contains a header specifying the geographic extent of the file and the layer definitions.</p>

<p>When exporting attributes as DXF text, point attributes may contain accented characters. However, the appropriate character encoding must be selected according to the application that will read the DXF file:</p>

<ul>
    <li>For AutoCAD 2000, FreeHand 8, and MicroStation 95, select ANSI-1252.</li>
    <li>For MS Word 2000 and CorelDraw 8, select OEM-850.</li>
</ul>

<p>In MicroStation 95, accented characters appear as asterisks after importing the DXF file. To correct this, change the font. One possible procedure is:</p>

<ul>
    <li>Zoom out to display the entire drawing.</li>
    <li>Use the "Fence" tool to select all the text.</li>
    <li>In the Text Tools window, click "Change Text Attributes".</li>
    <li>Select a font that supports Windows-ANSI accented characters, such as 105-INTL_ISO.</li>
    <li>Select "Use Fence".</li>
    <li>Click within the area selected by the Fence tool.</li>
</ul>

<p>The application allows you to apply scaling and coordinate offsets. This makes it possible to assign ground coordinates to a DXF file that does not already have them or to shift the entire drawing to a smaller coordinate range (for example, some applications may have difficulty opening DXF files containing UTM coordinates expressed in meters).</p>

<p>Notes:</p>

<ul>
    <li>Numeric or alphanumeric attribute values become layer names in the DXF file. In accordance with the DXF format specified by Autodesk, layer names are converted to uppercase and truncated to 31 characters. Characters not supported by the DXF format are replaced as appropriate (for example, 'À' becomes 'A', and '$' becomes '_').</li>
    <li>For files linked to a database, only one attribute can be exported. We recommend exporting the ID_GRAFIC field if the file is intended to be imported back into MiraMon. This allows all attributes to be restored using the "Physical Join" command in MiraDades.</li>
    <li>Negative numeric attribute values retain their minus sign in the layer name.</li>
    <li>Supported range for integer numeric attributes: -2147483648 to 2147483647 (long).</li>
    <li>Real-number attributes can only be exported as text because layer names cannot contain decimal points. If export as a layer name is forced, the decimal point is replaced with an underscore.</li>
    <li>Attributes can be omitted, placing all elements on a single layer. When exporting as text, all DXF text elements are placed on a single layer, and the attribute value becomes the text content.</li>
</ul>

